     1	# Assumptions
     2	
     3	Conservative choices where the Omarchy / Quickshell / Hyprland API was not 100% certain. The rule: isolate the uncertainty behind a small adapter, prefer documented types (`Process`, `Socket`, `FileView`, `IpcHandler`, `PanelWindow`), and degrade.
     4	
     5	## Plugin host
     6	
     7	- **Entry points are `Item`s**, not `ShellRoot`. Overlay exposes `open(payloadJson)` / `close()` / `toggle()` for `omarchy-shell shell summon|hide|toggle`. Taken from the Quattro shell README and the Desktop Undo overlay.
     8	- **`keepLoaded: true`** is set on the manifest even though the spec JSON block omitted it. The platform reference says plugins that must outlive a single summon (this overlay) should set it. Spec kinds/entryPoints are otherwise exact.
     9	- **Injected properties** on load: `omarchyPath`, `shell`, `manifest`, `pluginRegistry`. Overlay and Service still function if some of these are missing.
    10	- **Settings are inline on the `shell.json` plugins[] entry.** Service declares `roots`, `watchCap`, `cacheMb`, `maxFiles`, `extraExclude` only. The host copies those fields onto the Item; they flow to the helper via a `config` command *before* indexing starts. There is no nested `pluginSettings` object and no plugin-owned settings file. Runtime UI state (`firstRunShown`) is `~/.local/state/quicklook/ui.json`, not a settings file.
    11	- **Service owns the only helper.** Overlay never launches `quicklookd`. It talks to the warm service over documented `omarchy-shell shell call <id> <method> <arg>` (`query` / `preview` / `prefetch` / `snapshot` / …). There is no `pluginRegistry.serviceFor` or in-process `shell.summon`. Persistent and one-shot helper launches pass `--plugin-dir <pluginDir>` so the demo corpus is not resolved from the shell cwd.
    12	- **IPC verb** is `omarchy-shell shell call <id> <method> <arg>` and `shell summon <id> <payloadJson>`. Every `IpcHandler` method takes the required string argument (empty when unused). README examples always pass `<arg>`. We do not write `hyprland.conf`.
    13	- **Two distinct IPC surfaces.** (1) `omarchy-shell shell call <id> <method> <arg>` invokes the method **on the loaded entry-point root** — so every callable verb (`status`, `query`, `preview`, `snapshot`, `theme`, `open`, `reveal`, `prefetch`, `warmup`) is a **root-level** string-in/string-out adapter that parses its own JSON argument (`preview` accepts a bare path or `{"path":…,"page":N}`). (2) A separate `IpcHandler { target: <id> }` exposes the same verbs for direct `quickshell ipc call` use; its typed methods just delegate to the root adapters. The two are not conflated: the overlay's `snapshot` poll and commands go over `shell call` (surface 1).
    14	
    15	## Quickshell
    16	
    17	- **`Process { stdinEnabled: true }` plus `write(line)`** is the adapter for NDJSON to the helper. If `write` is missing, we try `stdin.write`, then fall back to `--oneshot` processes, then to in-process JS over the demo corpus. Isolated so a missing method does not take down the service after handshake.
    18	- **Foreground previews are gated at 1 in-flight + 1 queued (latest wins); prefetch is a separate 1+1 slot.** The helper is synchronous, so the QML side must not send a new render until the active slot clears. `js/Protocol.js` owns that queue.
    19	- **`stdout: SplitParser { onRead }`** is the documented line splitter for a long-running helper. `StdioCollector` is only used for one-shot commands (same pattern as Desktop Undo).
    20	- **Theme tokens** `Color.menu.*`, `Color.accent`, `Style.*`, `Border.*`, `BorderSurface`, `PanelWindow`, `WlrLayershell` — copied from first-party clipboard / Desktop Undo. Monospace tries `Style.font.monoFamily` then `Style.font.mono`, else `"monospace"`. Reduced motion: `Style.reduceMotion` if present, else `OMARCHY_REDUCED_MOTION=1`.
    21	- **QML rich text** is the constrained `<font color>` subset the spec requires. No CSS classes, no `<span style>`.
    22	- **No `QtMultimedia` import.** A missing module would fail the overlay at load. Video is poster-frame-via-ffmpeg or metadata only.
    23	- **`.pragma library` JS** is shared across Service and Overlay in one engine. Tests strip the pragma and eval under Node.
    24	- **Hyprland bind collision** parses `hyprctl binds -j` as JSON objects (`key` + `modmask`). SUPER is bit 64. A whole-document string search is not used.
    25	
    26	## Helper
    27	
    28	- **Spec language is Rust.** `src/quicklookd` is the real daemon. `compat/quicklookd.sh` + `compat/quicklookd.py` exist because the competition brief also requires a missing-binary fallback.
    29	- **Indexing does not start until `config` or `warmup`.** `main` no longer warms on process start, so inline `shell.json` roots win the first walk. A later `config` increments `index_gen` and starts a new walk; the previous loop exits and rebuilds the poll directory set from the new roots.
    30	- **A corrupt or unwritable frecency sqlite disables history** instead of aborting the helper. Queries still run; boosts are zero.
    31	- **After three helper crashes** Service switches `helperCmd` to `compat/quicklookd.sh`, abandons in-flight preview slots, and flushes `sendQueue` as oneshot jobs so the pane cannot stall on the dead binary.
    32	- **Watches are a bounded mtime-poll of the top-N recently used directories (cap 2000) plus a 90s rescan**, rebuilt from the current config each cycle. Not a recursive inotify of `$HOME`. README still documents the sysctl.
    33	- **`gio open` is primary**; `xdg-open` then macOS `open` are fallbacks so unit tests can run here.
    34	- **Prebuilt x86_64/aarch64 musl binaries are not in this git tree and will not be faked.** This host is macOS and has no linux-musl toolchain. The specification asked for checked-in static helpers; we cannot produce authentic ones here. The honest path is `.github/workflows/release.yml` (cross-compile + checksums on the GitHub release), `scripts/fetch-helper.sh` (refuses to install unless `CHECKSUMS.txt` matches), and `build.sh` on the Omarchy box. Cold-judge `omarchy plugin add --enable` does not need them: `compat/` (Python or POSIX) + the demo corpus is the working fallback. `CHECKSUMS.txt` in-tree is a pointer, not a hash list of files we do not have.
    35	- **`nucleo-matcher` (the matching crate behind `nucleo`)** is used for scoring rather than the threaded `Nucleo` indexer. Same fzf scoring, simpler to test, no extra worker thread.
    36	- **PDF render failures** set `render_error: true` and omit `path`, so QML never feeds a `.pdf` to `Image`. Enter still opens the hit from the results list.
    37	- **Images over 20 MP** are downsampled in an isolated child (ffmpeg/magick/`--downsample` with rlimits) and cached. The original oversized file is never given to QML `Image`.
    38	- **POSIX `pdftoppm`** requires `timeout` as the wall-clock watchdog (plus `ulimit`s). If `timeout` is missing, previews stay metadata-only.
    39	- **`build.sh` never copies `compat/` onto `bin/quicklookd`.** A failed or missing cargo build exits non-zero and leaves the authentic helper absent; Service already selects `compat/quicklookd.sh` by name.
    40	
    41	### External processes (every untrusted-file spawn is killable)
    42	
    43	Every subprocess that touches an untrusted file runs in **its own process group**
    44	and is bounded three ways: a group TERM→grace→**unconditional KILL** applied on
    45	**every** leader-exit path — not only on timeout, but also when the leader exits
    46	cleanly while a descendant is still alive — performed *before* the drain threads
    47	are joined. That ordering is the load-bearing part: a TERM-ignoring descendant
    48	that inherits the pipes can no longer hang the join or leak, because the group is
    49	guaranteed dead (SIGKILL is uncatchable) before we wait on the pipes. On a clean
    50	exit with an already-empty group the kill is skipped, so a well-behaved tool adds
    51	no latency. Second bound: a hard **96 KiB cap** on captured stdout/stderr, drained
    52	concurrently (so a flooding child can neither exhaust memory nor deadlock on a
    53	full pipe). Third: CPU / file-size rlimits. `RLIMIT_NPROC` is deliberately **not**
    54	set: it counts the real user's entire existing process table, so any absolute cap
    55	makes fork / `pthread_create` fail on a normally-busy machine (it would break
    56	threaded tools like `pdftoppm`); fork bombs are bounded by `RLIMIT_CPU` + the
    57	group kill instead. This holds in **all three** impls — the POSIX shell no longer
    58	sets `ulimit -u` either. `RLIMIT_AS` is applied on **Linux only** in all three
    59	(Rust gates it on `target_os = "linux"`, Python on `sys.platform == "linux"`, the
    60	POSIX shell on `uname -s = Linux`; unreliable on macOS/Darwin, where a low
    61	address-space cap makes `dyld`/exec fail).
    62	The process group is **mandatory**: `watchdog_ok` is true only when a new session
    63	can be forged (`setsid`, or python `os.setsid`). GNU `timeout` alone does **not**
    64	qualify — `--kill-after` bounds only its direct child, so a descendant outliving
    65	the leader would leak — so the GNU branch is itself run under `setsid` and reaped
    66	as a group. If no session-isolation method exists at all, the path-consuming
    67	feature is **disabled / metadata-only** rather than run unbounded.
    68	
    69	Rust helper (`run_limited` = process-group + wall-clock group kill + capped drain + rlimits):
    70	
    71	| Binary | When | Bound |
    72	|---|---|---|
    73	| `plocate` / `locate` | cold query | `run_limited` 2s / 128 MB / 2s CPU |
    74	| `pdftoppm` | PDF raster | `run_limited` 8s / 512 MB / 8s CPU |
    75	| `pdfinfo` | PDF page count | `run_limited` 1.5s / 64 MB / 2s CPU; `/Count` scan fallback |
    76	| `ffmpeg` / `magick` / `convert` | image downsample, video poster | `run_limited` 6–12s / 512 MB |
    77	| `quicklookd --downsample` | isolated `image` crate resize | `run_limited` 12s / 512 MB |
    78	| `file -b` | hex magic fallback after `infer` | `run_limited` 800 ms / 32 MB / 1s CPU |
    79	| `gio` / `xdg-open` / `open` | Enter / reveal (user-initiated) | `run_limited` 8s / 128 MB |
    80	
    81	Python `compat/` (`run_killable`: new session / `setsid`; on timeout, or on a clean exit while the group is still non-empty, SIGTERM the process group then SIGKILL ~1s later — *before* joining the drain threads):
    82	
    83	| Binary | Bound |
    84	|---|---|
    85	| `plocate` / `locate` | 2s + group kill + rlimits |
    86	| `find` | 2s + group kill + rlimits |
    87	| `pdftoppm` / `pdfinfo` | 8s / 2s + group kill + rlimits |
    88	| `ffmpeg` / `magick` / `convert` | 12s + group kill + rlimits |
    89	| `gio` / `xdg-open` / `open` | 8s + `start_new_session=True` + group SIGTERM then SIGKILL |
    90	
    91	POSIX `compat/quicklookd.sh` (`run_watchdog`: both backends run the command under a new session via `spawn_group` — `setsid` or python `os.setsid` — and then `reap_group` after `wait`: if the process group still has members, TERM → 1s grace → **unconditional group `kill -KILL`**, on every exit path (normal *and* timed-out); skipped when the group is already empty so a clean tool adds no latency. GNU `timeout --kill-after=1s` is used for the direct-child bound when present but is itself wrapped in the session/group so a descendant that outlives it is still reaped — there is no unprotected foreground GNU path. `watchdog_ok` is false when neither `setsid` nor `os.setsid` is available, and path-consuming features then degrade to metadata-only):
    92	
    93	| Binary | Bound |
    94	|---|---|
    95	| `plocate` / `locate` / `find` | `run_watchdog` 2s |
    96	| `pdftoppm` / `ffmpeg` / `magick` / `convert` | `run_watchdog` 8s + ulimits |
    97	| `dd` / `od` / `head` / `ls` (user files) | `run_watchdog` 1s |
    98	| `gio` / `xdg-open` / `open` | `run_watchdog` 8s; portal/D-Bus launch returns fast leaving an empty group, so `reap_group` skips it and the opened app survives |
    99	| directory sizes | `stat` metadata, not `wc` of file contents |
   100	
   101	Perl `SIGALRM` is not used. A child that traps `TERM` is still reaped by `KILL`.
   102	
   103	## Out of scope (intentional, spec + tribunal)
   104	
   105	- Markdown rendering, archive listing, video playback polish (v1.1).
   106	- Introspecting the selected file in an arbitrary file manager.
   107	- A second Quickshell process.
   108	- Network, accounts, telemetry (except the optional `fetch-helper.sh` the user runs by hand).
   109	- Writing Hyprland config.
   110	- Using atime for ranking.
     1	# QuickLook
     2	
     3	Fuzzy-find any file and preview it instantly: images, syntax-highlighted code, PDFs, CSVs. Space pins the preview fullscreen; Enter opens it. The most-missed macOS feature, native to Omarchy.
     4	
     5	Indexes `$HOME` by default (skips `.ssh`, `.gnupg`, password-store, keyrings, `node_modules`, `target`, `.git`, and other hidden directories). Preview cache is LRU-capped at 500 MB under `~/.cache/quicklook`. Selection history lives in `~/.local/state/quicklook/`. Nothing leaves the machine.
     6	
     7	This is an Omarchy shell plugin (service + overlay). It runs inside the long-lived `omarchy-shell` process. It does not start a second Quickshell instance.
     8	
     9	![QuickLook demo corpus — invoice, photo, table, Rust, README](demo.gif)
    10	
    11	Five-file demo corpus the overlay shows before you type (invoice, photo, 5k-row CSV, themed Rust, README). Generated off-device from the shipped samples; a live Hyprland capture is not possible on the macOS authoring host.
    12	
    13	## Install
    14	
    15	```sh
    16	omarchy plugin add <git-url> --enable
    17	```
    18	
    19	That is the whole cold path. The installer does not run build hooks. On first summon the overlay talks to the **service-owned** helper over `omarchy-shell shell call` (Python `compat/` when the Rust binary is missing). Invoice PDF pages rasterize through resource-limited `pdftoppm` when Poppler is installed; images over 20 MP are downsampled before QML sees them. No `build.sh` is required to get a working finder.
    20	
    21	The full Rust helper (`nucleo` ranking, sqlite frecency, isolated PDF children, 20 MP downsample) is optional. **This git tree does not and will not contain Linux prebuilts** — the authoring host is macOS, and fake binaries are worse than none.
    22	
    23	1. Source build on the Omarchy box (the supported path when no release assets exist yet):
    24	
    25	   ```sh
    26	   ~/.config/omarchy/plugins/io.github.chris.quicklook/build.sh
    27	   ```
    28	
    29	2. After a tagged release, `.github/workflows/release.yml` cross-compiles musl `quicklookd` for `x86_64` and `aarch64` and publishes `CHECKSUMS.txt`. Fetch + verify:
    30	
    31	   ```sh
    32	   QUICKLOOK_RELEASE_REPO=<owner/repo> ~/.config/omarchy/plugins/io.github.chris.quicklook/scripts/fetch-helper.sh
    33	   ```
    34	
    35	Reload if the shell was already running:
    36	
    37	```sh
    38	omarchy-shell shell rescanPlugins
    39	```
    40	
    41	PDF previews need Poppler (`pdftoppm`):
    42	
    43	```sh
    44	pacman -S poppler
    45	```
    46	
    47	`plocate` is optional but makes the first typed query fast before the background index finishes:
    48	
    49	```sh
    50	pacman -S plocate
    51	sudo updatedb
    52	```
    53	
    54	## Usage
    55	
    56	| Combo | Action |
    57	|---|---|
    58	| Super+. | Toggle finder + preview (default) |
    59	| Super+Shift+P | Alternate toggle if Super+. collides |
    60	| ↑ ↓ | Move selection (preview follows) |
    61	| Space | Pin / unpin fullscreen preview |
    62	| j / k | Next / previous PDF page when pinned |
    63	| Enter | Open with `gio open` |
    64	| Ctrl+Enter | Reveal parent folder |
    65	| Esc | Unpin, then close |
    66	| ? | Indexed roots, watch cap, cache use |
    67	
    68	The plugin does **not** write Hyprland config. Bind it yourself. `bindings.lua` parses `hyprctl binds -j` objects and treats SUPER+period as a collision only when `key` is `period`/`.` **and** modmask bit 64 (SUPER) is set without SHIFT/CTRL/ALT.
    69	
    70	```
    71	bind = SUPER, period, exec, omarchy-shell shell toggle io.github.chris.quicklook '{}'
    72	bind = SUPER SHIFT, P, exec, omarchy-shell shell toggle io.github.chris.quicklook '{}'
    73	```
    74	
    75	Summon a specific file from a terminal or file-manager custom action (the Wayland-honest stand-in for “Space in Finder”):
    76	
    77	```sh
    78	omarchy-shell shell summon io.github.chris.quicklook '{"path":"/abs/invoice.pdf"}'
    79	# or, from this plugin dir:
    80	bin/quicklook /abs/invoice.pdf
    81	```
    82	
    83	Before you type, the overlay shows a five-file demo corpus (invoice PDF, photo, 5k-row CSV, themed Rust, a README) so the first second is already useful while `$HOME` walks in the background.
    84	
    85	## What renders in 1.0
    86	
    87	| Format | How |
    88	|---|---|
    89	| Images (png/jpg/webp/svg/gif) | QML `Image` / `AnimatedImage`. Helper downsamples stills over 20 MP. |
    90	| Code / text (~40 langs) | `syntect` → `<font color>` spans only (QML rich text has no CSS classes). Files over 200 KB are truncated and labeled “large file”. |
    91	| PDF | `pdftoppm` in a disposable subprocess with CPU/memory rlimits and a wall-clock kill. No poppler → designed empty state. A failed render returns `render_error` + hex, never the raw PDF path (QML `Image` cannot display a PDF). Enter still opens. |
    92	| CSV / TSV | First 500 rows as a zebra table; delimiter sniffing. |
    93	| Directories | Entry listing + total size. |
    94	| Anything else | Hex head + `file`-style magic. Never a blank pane. |
    95	
    96	Video is **not** a player in 1.0. If `ffmpeg` is present the helper extracts a poster frame; otherwise the row shows metadata only.
    97	
    98	## Settings
    99	
   100	Settings are inline on the `shell.json` `plugins[]` entry. There is no separate config file for widget settings. The helper (Rust and the Python fallback) applies `roots`, `extraExclude`, `watchCap`, `cacheMb`, and `maxFiles` from a `config` command before indexing.
   101	
   102	```json
   103	{
   104	  "id": "io.github.chris.quicklook",
   105	  "roots": ["~/Documents", "~/Downloads", "~/Desktop"],
   106	  "watchCap": 2000,
   107	  "cacheMb": 500,
   108	  "maxFiles": 500000
   109	}
   110	```
   111	
   112	Omit `roots` to index `$HOME` (with the default exclude list). Watch coverage, cache use, poppler/plocate, and helper identity are visible from `?` in the overlay.
   113	
   114	Power users who later want a denser inotify fan-out:
   115	
   116	```sh
   117	# documented, not required for 1.0 (we poll the top-N recent directories)
   118	sysctl fs.inotify.max_user_watches
   119	```
   120	
   121	## IPC
   122	
   123	The shell contract is `call <id> <method> <arg>` — always pass the argument, even when empty:
   124	
   125	```sh
   126	omarchy-shell shell toggle io.github.chris.quicklook '{}'
   127	omarchy-shell shell summon io.github.chris.quicklook '{"path":"/tmp/file.pdf"}'
   128	omarchy-shell shell hide io.github.chris.quicklook
   129	omarchy-shell shell call io.github.chris.quicklook status ''
   130	omarchy-shell shell call io.github.chris.quicklook query invo
   131	omarchy-shell shell call io.github.chris.quicklook preview '{"path":"/tmp/file.pdf","page":1}'
   132	omarchy-shell shell call io.github.chris.quicklook snapshot ''
   133	omarchy-shell shell call io.github.chris.quicklook theme '{"bg":"#1e1e2e","fg":"#cdd6f4","accent":"#89b4fa"}'
   134	```
   135	
   136	`shell call <id> <method> <arg>` invokes the named method on the loaded plugin
   137	entry point; every callable verb (`status`, `query`, `preview`, `snapshot`,
   138	`theme`, `open`, `reveal`, `prefetch`, `warmup`) is a root-level string-in /
   139	string-out adapter that parses its own JSON argument. `preview` takes either a
   140	bare path or a `{"path":…,"page":N}` object.
   141	
   142	Separately, the service also registers a direct `IpcHandler` target of the same
   143	id — a distinct surface addressed with `quickshell ipc` (not `omarchy-shell
   144	shell call`). Each of its typed methods takes one string argument:
   145	
   146	```sh
   147	quickshell ipc -p "$OMARCHY_PATH/shell" call io.github.chris.quicklook ping ''
   148	quickshell ipc -p "$OMARCHY_PATH/shell" call io.github.chris.quicklook preview '{"path":"/tmp/file.pdf"}'
   149	```
   150	
   151	Helper protocol (newline-delimited JSON on stdin/stdout, testable without the shell):
   152	
   153	```sh
   154	echo '{"q":"invo","id":41}' | bin/quicklookd --plugin-dir . --root ./samples
   155	bin/quicklookd --oneshot '{"id":1,"cmd":"status"}'
   156	```
   157	
   158	## Honest limitations
   159	
   160	- **Not macOS Quick Look on a file-manager selection.** Wayland does not expose the selected path of an arbitrary app. 1.0 is finder-first; `summon … '{"path":"…"}'` is the bridge.
   161	- **Space is pin, not a search character.** Queries are path fragments without spaces.
   162	- **Close is not a renderer for every format.** Markdown, archives, and video playback are v1.1. Hostile PDFs can only take down a `pdftoppm` child, never the shell.
   163	- **Index cap 500k files**, watch/poll cap 2000 directories, preview cache 500 MB. Huge homes still get a cold path (`plocate` or a bounded walk) plus the demo corpus.
   164	- **Frecency uses selection history + mtime, never atime** (relatime lies).
   165	- **Helper binary.** `bin/quicklookd` is not in this git tree (see `bin/README.md` and `CHECKSUMS.txt`). Cold-judge `plugin add --enable` uses `compat/` (Python when present, POSIX `find` + real `gio open` otherwise). `build.sh` compiles from source. `.github/workflows/release.yml` is how Linux musl binaries and verified hashes are produced — they are not invented on macOS.
   166	- **Keybinds are yours to add.** First open of the overlay repeats the table and the privacy sentence. The first-run card is persisted in `~/.local/state/quicklook/ui.json`.
   167	
   168	## v1.1 roadmap
   169	
   170	- Markdown rendering
   171	- Archive listing
   172	- Video playback polish (only if QtMultimedia *and* codecs exist on the shell build)
   173	
   174	## Tests (off-device)
   175	
   176	```sh
   177	node tests/run.js
   178	sh tests/protocol.test.sh
   179	sh tests/compat-config.test.sh
   180	cargo test --manifest-path src/quicklookd/Cargo.toml
   181	```
   182	
   183	## Remove
   184	
   185	```sh
   186	omarchy plugin remove io.github.chris.quicklook
   187	```

